      {
        "date": "2018",
        "tag": "Production",
        "t": "Continuously marked: Traceability from the extruder",
        "teaser": "Every K-Aqua pipe bears a continuous marking directly from the extrusion process.",
        "body": [
          "With up to 14,000 metres of pipe per day and line, traceability is not optional: every pipe is continuously marked during the extrusion process, with the details planners and inspectors need on site.",
          "The marking links the physical product to its batch and the quality assurance test reports. What is printed on the pipe can be traced back to the test labouratory, a component of the QA manual that auditors can request in the Trust Centre."
        ]
      },
      {
        "date": "Knowledge",
        "tag": "Material",
        "t": "Why PP-R anyway?",
        "teaser": "From flare gas to drinking water pipe: the short material history of polypropylene.",
        "body": [
          "Polypropylene is produced from gases that were previously flared off uselessly, its material use reduces air pollution at the source. As an engineering plastic, PP is unusually resistant to solvents, alkalis, and acids and forms complex geometries in injection molding.",
          "In a pipe, the inner surface counts most: with 0.007 mm roughness, pressure loss remains minimal, and biofilm and incrustation find almost no hold. Add to this a thermal conductivity of 0.24 W/(m·K), hot water stays hot without the pipe insulating against itself.",
          "PPRCT pushes the limits even further: the modified crystal structure brings more temperature and pressure reserve, SDR 7.4 holds 70 °C at 10 bar in continuous operation."
        ]
      },
      {
        "date": "Knowledge",
        "tag": "Material",
        "t": "Brass meets Polypropylene: Transitions into existing systems",
        "teaser": "Renovation almost always means a system change, transition pieces with brass threads make it clean.",
        "body": [
          "Hardly any project starts on a green field: existing pipes made of copper or galvanized steel must be connected. For this purpose, there are transition pieces with embedded brass female or male threads, the threaded side connects to the existing metal, the PP side is materially welded.",
          "This allows section-by-section renovation: corroded lines are replaced, functioning ones remain, and the corrosion problem doesn't migrate to the new system. Screw connections make the joint detachable if necessary, such as at valves and water metres."
        ]
      }
    ],
